                   [\n                        851.6,\n                        1753.5\n                    ],\n                    [\n                        851.6,\n                        2069.2\n                    ],\n                    [\n                        1555.6,\n                        2069.2\n                    ],\n                    [\n                        1555.6,\n                        1753.5\n                    ]\n                ],\n                \"system\": \"PixelSpace\"\n            },\n            \"detection_class_prob\": 0.9553,\n            \"file_directory\": \"/Users/amirshahcheraghian/AI Projects/personal-chatbot/src/business/rag/data\",\n            \"filename\": \"kthproject.pdf\",\n            \"filetype\": \"application/pdf\",\n            \"is_extracted\": \"true\",\n            \"languages\": [\n                \"eng\"\n            ],\n            \"last_modified\": \"2026-01-29T11:02:43\",\n            \"page_number\": 7\n        },\n        \"text\": \"Fig. 4 presents a map segmented into 10 districts using a k-means clustering algorithm accessed via the sci-kit-learn library. Each ten district is assigned the heat sources within its cluster and water body sources, which are connected based on the minimum connection dis- tance. The heat supply from each source is calculated, and the total cost is determined using the marginal cost of heat (MCOH). In Fig. 4, each bubble represents a heat load, with the bubble size corresponding to the annual energy demand. Table 4 provides each cluster\\u2019s total heat energy demand and the allocated residual heat supply by type. The total heating demand of 7.7 TWh/year matches the reported district heating supply of the city [34], indicating that the EPC dataset offers comprehensive\",\n        \"type\": \"NarrativeText\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"element_id\": \"f812809e1db4d7a877fa7d3f8c9801bd\",\n        \"metadata\": {\n            \"coordinates\": {\n                \"layout_heig